Subject: [PATCH] nsresourced: attach namespace retirement through BTF fentry

Secure Boot lockdown rejects kprobe perf events. Attach the same retirement
notification to the same typed function through the BPF trampoline instead.
Keep attachment failure fatal to managed user namespace availability; there
is no kprobe fallback or change to the restriction maps and ring buffer.

--- a/src/bpf/userns-restrict.bpf.c
+++ b/src/bpf/userns-restrict.bpf.c
@@ -276,12 +276,13 @@ int BPF_PROG(userns_restrict_task_fix_setgroups, struct cred *new_cred, const struct
         return 0;
 }
 
-SEC("kprobe/retire_userns_sysctls")
-int BPF_KPROBE(userns_restrict_retire_userns_sysctls, struct user_namespace *userns) {
+SEC("fentry/retire_userns_sysctls")
+int BPF_PROG(userns_restrict_retire_userns_sysctls, struct user_namespace *userns) {
         unsigned inode;
 
-        /* Inform userspace that a user namespace just went away. I wish there was a nicer way to hook into
-         * user namespaces being deleted than using kprobes, but couldn't find any. */
+        /* The selected kernel exposes this typed function in BTF. Unlike a kprobe perf event, its
+         * fentry attachment works with Secure Boot lockdown and retains the same notification point.
+         * Failure to load or attach still leaves managed user namespaces unavailable. */
         userns = bpf_rdonly_cast(userns, bpf_core_type_id_kernel(struct user_namespace));
         inode = userns->ns.inum;
 
